LG Display is developing a new way to make <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> screens with higher brightness, longer lifespans, and lower power consumption for future Macs, according to <a href="https://www.thelec.net/news/articleView.html?idxno=15762"><em>The Elec</em></a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/03/macbook-air-new-blue.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-939665" /><br/>
The technology, called "FLiPP," is reportedly being developed for potential use in Apple's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-air/">MacBook Air</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> in 2030. LG unveiled it in August, but the latest report identifies the Apple products it could eventually reach.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;OLED&zwnj; screens contain tiny pixels that produce their own light. LG's new process reduces the empty space between them, allowing more of the screen's surface to light up. <a href="https://m.lgdisplay.com/kor/company/media-center/latest-news?contentId=5575">LG says</a> manufacturers could use that improvement to make screens 60% brighter, last 2.4 times as long, or consume 13% less power than &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; screens made using the conventional process.<br/>
<br/>
Producing the displays could also become cheaper. FLiPP replaces the thin metal sheets normally used to arrange pixels, allowing LG to make more screens from each sheet of glass and waste less material.<br/>
<br/>
<em>Bloomberg</em>'s <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mark-gurman/">Mark Gurman</a> said last month that Apple was still <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/25/oled-macbook-air-and-imac-in-testing/">testing OLED displays</a> for the &zwnj;MacBook Air&zwnj; and &zwnj;iMac&zwnj;. However, <em>The Elec</em> also <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/19/apple-may-cancel-oled-imac/">today reported</a> that Apple could delay or cancel its &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;iMac&zwnj; because of costs and demand concerns, potentially affecting LG's plans.<div class="linkback">Related Roundups: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-air/">MacBook Air</a></div><div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/lg-display/">LG Display</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/oled/">OLED</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/the-elec/">The Elec</a></div><div class="linkback">Buyer's Guide: <a href="https://buyersguide.macrumors.com/#iMac">iMac (Buy Now)</a>, <a href="https://buyersguide.macrumors.com/#MacBook_Air">MacBook Air (Caution)</a></div><div class="linkback">Related Forums: <a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/forums/imac.91">iMac</a>, <a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/forums/macbook-air.122">MacBook Air</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/19/future-macs-could-get-brighter-oled-displays/">Future Macs Could Get Brighter, Longer-Lasting OLED Displays in 2030</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/future-macs-could-get-brighter-longer-lasting-oled-displays-in-2030.2494800/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>